NNAAMMEE
     ggrroowwffss — grow size of an existing ffs file system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ggrroowwffss [--NNqqyy] [--ss _s_i_z_e] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggrroowwffss utility extends the newfs(8) program.  Before starting ggrroowwffss,
     the partition must be set to a larger size using disklabel(8).  The
     ggrroowwffss utility extends the size of the file system on the specified spe‐
     cial file.

     Currently ggrroowwffss can only enlarge unmounted file systems.  Do not try en‐
     larging a mounted file system - your system may panic and you will not be
     able to use the file system any longer.  Most of the newfs(8) options
     cannot be changed by ggrroowwffss.  In fact, you can only increase the size of
     the file system.  Use tunefs(8) for other changes.

     The following options are available:

     --NN         Test mode.  Causes the new file system parameters to be
                printed out without actually enlarging the file system.

     --qq         Operate in quiet mode.  With this option, ggrroowwffss will not
                print extraneous information like superblock backups.

     --ss _s_i_z_e    Determines the _s_i_z_e of the file system after enlarging in sec‐
                tors.  This value defaults to the size of the raw partition
                specified in _s_p_e_c_i_a_l (in other words, ggrroowwffss will enlarge the
                file system to the size of the entire partition).

     --yy         Expert mode.  Usually ggrroowwffss will ask you if you have taken a
                backup of your data and will test whether _s_p_e_c_i_a_l is currently
                mounted.  The --yy flag suppresses this, so use this option with
                great care!

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     COLUMNS  If set to a positive integer, output is formatted to the given
              width in columns.  Otherwise, ggrroowwffss defaults to the terminal
              width, or 80 columns if the output is not a terminal.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     disklabel(8), dumpfs(8), fdisk(8), fsck(8), newfs(8), tunefs(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ggrroowwffss utility first appeared in FreeBSD 4.4 and has been available
     since OpenBSD 3.4.

